(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("aiops",{"aiops/what-aiops-is-and-is-not":{"html":"\u003cp>AIOps means using algorithms to help run systems: spotting unusual metrics, grouping noisy alerts, pointing at likely causes and automating routine fixes. The promise is less noise and faster recovery. The risk is a black box that adds a new thing to debug. This lesson sets realistic expectations so you can adopt the useful parts and skip the hype.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Define AIOps and list its common uses\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Separate problems algorithms solve well from those they do not\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Explain why good telemetry comes before any AIOps tool\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Judge a tool by measurable results\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-it-covers\">What it covers\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Four jobs appear again and again: anomaly detection on metrics and logs, alert correlation and noise reduction, root cause hints, and automated or assisted remediation. Newer tools add language models that summarise incidents and search runbooks. Each job is a separate problem with its own limits.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-it-needs-first\">What it needs first\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Algorithms can only learn from the data you give them. If metrics are missing, logs are unstructured, service names are inconsistent and changes are not recorded, no tool can recover the picture. Clean telemetry, a service catalogue and a change log are the foundation. Doing that work first often removes much of the need.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"where-it-helps\">Where it helps\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>It helps where humans cannot keep up: thousands of metrics with daily patterns, alert storms that bury the one that matters, and repetitive checks during an incident. It struggles where the system is new, behaviour changes constantly, or the failure has never happened before.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"where-it-does-not\">Where it does not\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A model cannot know your business rules, a planned launch or why traffic doubled. It cannot replace on-call judgement. It can mislead when it sounds confident. Treat every output as a hint with a reason you can check, not an answer.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"judging-a-tool\">Judging a tool\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Define measures before you start: alerts per incident, time to acknowledge, time to find the cause, false positives, missed incidents. Run it beside your current process for a few weeks. Keep it if the numbers improve and engineers trust it. Drop it if they ignore it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-baseline-you-can-measure\">A baseline you can measure\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"./incident_stats.sh --from 2026-01-01 --to 2026-03-31\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>./incident_stats.sh --from 2026-01-01 --to 2026-03-31\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">incidents:                       42\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">alerts fired:                    6180\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">alerts per incident (median):    71\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">pages per week per person:       19\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">time to acknowledge (median):    6 min\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">time to identify cause (median): 38 min\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">false pages (no action needed):  57%\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Numbers like these are what a tool must improve. Without a baseline, nobody can say whether it helped. The output is a simulated illustration.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-it-fails\">How it fails\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A tool is bought before telemetry is fixed. Results are not measured, so people argue from opinion. A model flags everything as unusual and is muted. A confident suggestion is trusted without checking.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4;n4&gt;n5;n5&gt;n6\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How What AIOps is and is not fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"8.3\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"25.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"25.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"41.7\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"41.7\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"58.3\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"58.3\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"75.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"75.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"91.7\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:8.3%;top:25.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">What it covers\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:25.0%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">What it needs first\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:41.7%;top:25.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Where it helps\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:58.3%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Where it does not\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n5\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:75.0%;top:25.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Judging a tool\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n6\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:91.7%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">A baseline you can measure\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, What it covers to What it needs first; 2, What it needs first to Where it helps; 3, Where it helps to Where it does not; 4, Where it does not to Judging a tool; 5, Judging a tool to A baseline you can measure.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>What it covers \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Four jobs appear again and again: anomaly detection on metrics and logs, alert correlation and noise reduction, root cause hints, and automated or assisted remediation.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>What it needs first \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Algorithms can only learn from the data you give them.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Buying a tool before fixing telemetry.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Where it helps \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> It helps where humans cannot keep up: thousands of metrics with daily patterns, alert storms that bury the one that matters, and repetitive checks during an incident.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Where it helps It helps where humans cannot keep up: thousands of metrics with daily patterns, alert storms that bury the one that matters...\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Where it does not \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A model cannot know your business rules, a planned launch or why traffic doubled.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Trusting confident suggestions without checking.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n5\">\u003cdt>Judging a tool \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Define measures before you start: alerts per incident, time to acknowledge, time to find the cause, false positives, missed incidents.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n6\">\u003cdt>A baseline you can measure \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Numbers like these are what a tool must improve.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Having no baseline to compare against.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-tool-that-learned-the-wrong-normal\">Incident: A tool that learned the wrong normal\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A team trained an anomaly detector on three quiet weeks. When a busy sales period began, it flagged every metric as abnormal. The on-call engineers muted it, and later a real fault went unnoticed.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aiops-tool status\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aiops-tool status\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">anomalies open: 412\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">training window: 2026-02-02 to 2026-02-22\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">note: no seasonal patterns learned\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The output is a simulated illustration. A detector must learn from a window that includes normal busy and quiet periods, and its alert volume must be watched like any other service, or it becomes noise itself.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"interview-question\">Interview question\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>&quot;Would you buy an AIOps platform?&quot; Start with the problem and a baseline, check that telemetry and change data are good, trial it beside current practice, and measure alert noise, time to cause and false positives before deciding.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Buying a tool before fixing telemetry\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Having no baseline to compare against\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Trusting confident suggestions without checking\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Training a detector on an unrepresentative window\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"what-it-covers","text":"What it covers"},{"id":"what-it-needs-first","text":"What it needs first"},{"id":"where-it-helps","text":"Where it helps"},{"id":"where-it-does-not","text":"Where it does not"},{"id":"judging-a-tool","text":"Judging a tool"},{"id":"a-baseline-you-can-measure","text":"A baseline you can measure"},{"id":"how-it-fails","text":"How it fails"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-a-tool-that-learned-the-wrong-normal","text":"Incident: A tool that learned the wrong normal"},{"id":"interview-question","text":"Interview question"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Google SRE book: Monitoring distributed systems","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/monitoring-distributed-systems/"},{"t":"Google SRE workbook: Alerting on SLOs","u":"https://sre.google/workbook/alerting-on-slos/"}],"text":"AIOps means using algorithms to help run systems: spotting unusual metrics, grouping noisy alerts, pointing at likely causes and automating routine fixes. The promise is less noise and faster recovery. The risk is a black box that adds a new thing to debug. This lesson sets realistic expectations so you can adopt the useful parts and skip the hype. What you will learn Define AIOps and list its common uses Separate problems algorithms solve well from those they do not Explain why good telemetry comes before any AIOps tool Judge a tool by measurable results What it covers Four jobs appear again and again: anomaly detection on metrics and logs, alert correlation and noise reduction, root cause hints, and automated or assisted remediation. Newer tools add language models that summarise incidents and search runbooks. Each job is a separate problem with its own limits. What it needs first Algorithms can only learn from the data you give them. If metrics are missing, logs are unstructured, service names are inconsistent and changes are not recorded, no tool can recover the picture. Clean telemetry, a service catalogue and a change log are the foundation. Doing that work first often removes much of the need. Where it helps It helps where humans cannot keep up: thousands of metrics with daily patterns, alert storms that bury the one that matters, and repetitive checks during an incident. It struggles where the system is new, behaviour changes constantly, or the failure has never happened before. Where it does not A model cannot know your business rules, a planned launch or why traffic doubled. It cannot replace on-call judgement. It can mislead when it sounds confident. Treat every output as a hint with a reason you can check, not an answer. Judging a tool Define measures before you start: alerts per incident, time to acknowledge, time to find the cause, false positives, missed incidents. Run it beside your current process for a few weeks. Keep it if the numbers improve and engineers trust it. Drop it if they ignore it. A baseline you can measure $ ./incident_stats.sh --from 2026-01-01 --to 2026-03-31 incidents: 42 alerts fired: 6180 alerts per incident (median): 71 pages per week per person: 19 time to acknowledge (median): 6 min time to identify cause (median): 38 min false pages (no action needed): 57% Numbers like these are what a tool must improve. Without a baseline, nobody can say whether it helped. The output is a simulated illustration. How it fails A tool is bought before telemetry is fixed. Results are not measured, so people argue from opinion. A model flags everything as unusual and is muted. A confident suggestion is trusted without checking. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How What AIOps is and is not fits together Flow in order: 1, What it covers to What it needs first; 2, What it needs first to Where it helps; 3, Where it helps to Where it does not; 4, Where it does not to Judging a tool; 5, Judging a tool to A baseline you can measure. Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. What it covers Step What it does: Four jobs appear again and again: anomaly detection on metrics and logs, alert correlation and noise reduction, root cause hints, and automated or assisted remediation. If it fails or is weak: Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here. What it needs first Step What it does: Algorithms can only learn from the data you give them. If it fails or is weak: Buying a tool before fixing telemetry. Where it helps Step What it does: It helps where humans cannot keep up: thousands of metrics with daily patterns, alert storms that bury the one that matters, and repetitive checks during an incident. If it fails or is weak: Where it helps It helps whe"},"aiops/anomaly-detection-on-metrics":{"html":"\u003cp>Fixed thresholds fail when normal changes by the hour: a CPU of 70 percent is fine at noon and alarming at 4am. Anomaly detection learns what normal looks like and flags departures. The simplest methods work better than most people expect, and they are easy to explain, which matters when a human must trust the alert at night.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Compare static thresholds with learned baselines\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Explain seasonality and why it matters\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Apply a rolling mean and standard deviation, and a seasonal baseline\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reduce false alarms with duration, smoothing and context\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"why-static-thresholds-struggle\">Why static thresholds struggle\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A single limit ignores daily and weekly rhythm. Set it low and it fires every busy hour. Set it high and it misses a quiet-hour fault. Teams then keep adjusting it, or give up and ignore it. A learned baseline moves with the pattern.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"rolling-baselines\">Rolling baselines\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Compute the average and spread of a metric over a recent window, then flag values many spreads away. This z-score approach is cheap and easy to explain. It copes poorly with strong daily patterns, because last hour is not a good guide to this hour.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"seasonality\">Seasonality\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Compare the metric with the same time on previous days or weeks. A Monday 9am value is judged against other Monday 9am values. This single idea removes most false alarms for traffic-like metrics. Add known events, such as launches and holidays, as context.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cut-false-alarms\">Cut false alarms\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Require the anomaly to last for several minutes, smooth spikes with a median, set a minimum size so tiny absolute changes are ignored, and alert on symptoms users feel, not every internal metric. Review each alert after the fact: fix the rule or delete it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"when-to-use-models\">When to use models\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>If simple seasonal baselines are not enough, forecasting models or isolation forests can help with many series at once. Start simple, compare the complex approach on past incidents, and keep it only if it finds real problems earlier without adding noise.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-seasonal-baseline-in-promql\">A seasonal baseline in PromQL\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"promql\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# Requests per second now, compared with the same time last week\n(\n  sum(rate(http_requests_total{job=&quot;checkout&quot;}[5m]))\n  /\n  sum(rate(http_requests_total{job=&quot;checkout&quot;}[5m] offset 1w))\n)\n# Alert when traffic falls below 60 percent of last week for 10 minutes\n# (sum(rate(http_requests_total{job=&quot;checkout&quot;}[5m]))\n#   / sum(rate(http_requests_total{job=&quot;checkout&quot;}[5m] offset 1w))) &lt; 0.6\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># Requests per second now, compared with the same time last week\u003c/span>\n(\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sum\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">rate\u003c/span>(http_requests_total{\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">job\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;checkout&quot;\u003c/span>}[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">5\u003c/span>m]))\n  /\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sum\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">rate\u003c/span>(http_requests_total{\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">job\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;checkout&quot;\u003c/span>}[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">5\u003c/span>m] \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">offset\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span>w))\n)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># Alert when traffic falls below 60 percent of last week for 10 minutes\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># (sum(rate(http_requests_total{job=&quot;checkout&quot;}[5m]))\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\">#   / sum(rate(http_requests_total{job=&quot;checkout&quot;}[5m] offset 1w))) &lt; 0.6\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Comparing with the same time last week removes the daily pattern. Pair it with a minimum traffic floor so a quiet hour does not create false alarms.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-it-fails\">How it fails\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A model is trained on an unusual period. A launch or holiday looks like an attack. Alerts fire on tiny changes in low-traffic services. Nobody reviews the detector, so false alarms pile up until it is ignored.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4;n4&gt;n5;n5&gt;n6\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Anomaly detection on metrics fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"8.3\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"25.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"25.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"41.7\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"41.7\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"58.3\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"58.3\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"75.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"75.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"91.7\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:8.3%;top:25.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Why static thresholds struggle\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:25.0%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Rolling baselines\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:41.7%;top:25.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Seasonality\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:58.3%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Cut false alarms\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n5\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:75.0%;top:25.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">When to use models\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n6\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:91.7%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">A seasonal baseline in PromQL\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Why static thresholds struggle to Rolling baselines; 2, Rolling baselines to Seasonality; 3, Seasonality to Cut false alarms; 4, Cut false alarms to When to use models; 5, When to use models to A seasonal baseline in PromQL.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Why static thresholds struggle \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A single limit ignores daily and weekly rhythm.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Comparing with the previous hour only.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Rolling baselines \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Compute the average and spread of a metric over a recent window, then flag values many spreads away.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Seasonality \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Compare the metric with the same time on previous days or weeks.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Ignoring holidays and launches.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Cut false alarms \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Require the anomaly to last for several minutes, smooth spikes with a median, set a minimum size so tiny absolute changes are ignored, and alert on symptoms users feel...\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n5\">\u003cdt>When to use models \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> If simple seasonal baselines are not enough, forecasting models or isolation forests can help with many series at once.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Never reviewing past alerts.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n6\">\u003cdt>A seasonal baseline in PromQL \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Comparing with the same time last week removes the daily pattern.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-monday-that-looked-like-an-outage\">Incident: The Monday that looked like an outage\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A detector compared each hour with the previous hour. Every Monday morning, when traffic rose sharply, it raised a high-severity alert. On-call engineers learned to ignore Monday alerts, and one Monday a real fault was missed.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"detector explain --alert 4412\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>detector explain --alert 4412\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">metric: checkout_requests  value: 1840/s\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">compared with: previous hour mean 620/s (z = 6.2)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">seasonal expectation (same hour, last 4 Mondays): 1790/s (z = 0.3)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The output is a simulated illustration. Comparing with the same hour on previous Mondays would have shown normal behaviour. Showing both comparisons also made the alert explainable.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"interview-question\">Interview question\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>&quot;How would you alert on traffic anomalies?&quot; Compare with a seasonal baseline from the same time on earlier weeks, require the shift to last, ignore tiny absolute values, and review false alarms until the rule is quiet.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Comparing with the previous hour only\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Alerting on a single data point\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring holidays and launches\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Never reviewing past alerts\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"why-static-thresholds-struggle","text":"Why static thresholds struggle"},{"id":"rolling-baselines","text":"Rolling baselines"},{"id":"seasonality","text":"Seasonality"},{"id":"cut-false-alarms","text":"Cut false alarms"},{"id":"when-to-use-models","text":"When to use models"},{"id":"a-seasonal-baseline-in-promql","text":"A seasonal baseline in PromQL"},{"id":"how-it-fails","text":"How it fails"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-monday-that-looked-like-an-outage","text":"Incident: The Monday that looked like an outage"},{"id":"interview-question","text":"Interview question"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Prometheus: querying basics","u":"https://prometheus.io/docs/prometheus/latest/querying/basics/"},{"t":"Prometheus: alerting practices","u":"https://prometheus.io/docs/practices/alerting/"}],"text":"Fixed thresholds fail when normal changes by the hour: a CPU of 70 percent is fine at noon and alarming at 4am. Anomaly detection learns what normal looks like and flags departures. The simplest methods work better than most people expect, and they are easy to explain, which matters when a human must trust the alert at night. What you will learn Compare static thresholds with learned baselines Explain seasonality and why it matters Apply a rolling mean and standard deviation, and a seasonal baseline Reduce false alarms with duration, smoothing and context Why static thresholds struggle A single limit ignores daily and weekly rhythm. Set it low and it fires every busy hour. Set it high and it misses a quiet-hour fault. Teams then keep adjusting it, or give up and ignore it. A learned baseline moves with the pattern. Rolling baselines Compute the average and spread of a metric over a recent window, then flag values many spreads away. This z-score approach is cheap and easy to explain. It copes poorly with strong daily patterns, because last hour is not a good guide to this hour. Seasonality Compare the metric with the same time on previous days or weeks. A Monday 9am value is judged against other Monday 9am values. This single idea removes most false alarms for traffic-like metrics. Add known events, such as launches and holidays, as context. Cut false alarms Require the anomaly to last for several minutes, smooth spikes with a median, set a minimum size so tiny absolute changes are ignored, and alert on symptoms users feel, not every internal metric. Review each alert after the fact: fix the rule or delete it. When to use models If simple seasonal baselines are not enough, forecasting models or isolation forests can help with many series at once. Start simple, compare the complex approach on past incidents, and keep it only if it finds real problems earlier without adding noise. A seasonal baseline in PromQL # Requests per second now, compared with the same time last week ( sum ( rate (http_requests_total{ job = \"checkout\" }[ 5 m])) / sum ( rate (http_requests_total{ job = \"checkout\" }[ 5 m] offset 1 w)) ) # Alert when traffic falls below 60 percent of last week for 10 minutes # (sum(rate(http_requests_total{job=\"checkout\"}[5m])) # / sum(rate(http_requests_total{job=\"checkout\"}[5m] offset 1w))) \u003c 0.6 Comparing with the same time last week removes the daily pattern. Pair it with a minimum traffic floor so a quiet hour does not create false alarms. How it fails A model is trained on an unusual period. A launch or holiday looks like an attack. Alerts fire on tiny changes in low-traffic services. Nobody reviews the detector, so false alarms pile up until it is ignored. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Anomaly detection on metrics fits together Flow in order: 1, Why static thresholds struggle to Rolling baselines; 2, Rolling baselines to Seasonality; 3, Seasonality to Cut false alarms; 4, Cut false alarms to When to use models; 5, When to use models to A seasonal baseline in PromQL. Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Why static thresholds struggle Step What it does: A single limit ignores daily and weekly rhythm. If it fails or is weak: Comparing with the previous hour only. Rolling baselines Step What it does: Compute the average and spread of a metric over a recent window, then flag values many spreads away. If it fails or is weak: Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here. Seasonality Step What it does: Compare the metric with the same time on previous days or weeks. If it fails or is weak: Ignoring holidays and launches. Cut false alarms Step What it does: Require the anomaly to last for several minutes, smooth spikes with a median, set a minim"},"aiops/alert-correlation-and-noise-reduction":{"html":"\u003cp>During a real outage, one failing database can trigger hundreds of alerts from every service that depends on it. The person on call sees a wall of red and has to work out which alert is the cause and which are echoes. Correlation groups related alerts, and noise reduction removes the ones that never needed a human. Both can be done well with plain rules before adding machine learning.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Explain alert storms and why they slow response\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Deduplicate and group alerts using labels and time\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Use service dependencies to find the likely source\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Measure noise and track it down over time\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"why-storms-hurt\">Why storms hurt\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Each extra alert costs attention. When many fire at once, the engineer must read, sort and guess. The first alert is often the most useful, and it gets buried. A good system shows one incident with the likely cause on top and the echoes folded underneath.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"deduplicate-and-group\">Deduplicate and group\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Give every alert stable labels: service, environment, team and a fingerprint of the problem. Identical alerts collapse into one with a count. Alerts that share a service or cluster and start within a few minutes group together. This alone often cuts volume by more than half.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"use-topology\">Use topology\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>If you know that checkout calls payments, and payments calls the database, then a database fault explains alerts above it. Walk the dependency graph from the alerting services toward the shared dependency that is also unhealthy. Surface that one first and label the rest as downstream.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"inhibit-and-silence-by-design\">Inhibit and silence by design\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Inhibition rules suppress low-value alerts while a high-value one is active, for example, mute pod alerts when the whole node is down. Planned work should silence alerts with an expiry and a reason. Avoid permanent silences: they hide new problems.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"measure-and-prune\">Measure and prune\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Track alerts per incident, the share that led to an action, and alerts that always resolve alone. Retire or retune any alert that is mostly ignored. Noise is a debt that grows quietly, so give someone ownership of the number.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"grouping-and-inhibition-in-alertmanager\">Grouping and inhibition in Alertmanager\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"route:\n  receiver: team-pager\n  group_by: [cluster, service]\n  group_wait: 30s\n  group_interval: 5m\n  repeat_interval: 4h\ninhibit_rules:\n  - source_matchers: [alertname = &quot;NodeDown&quot;]\n    target_matchers: [severity = &quot;warning&quot;]\n    equal: [cluster, node]\n  - source_matchers: [alertname = &quot;DatabaseDown&quot;]\n    target_matchers: [alertname =~ &quot;Service.*Errors&quot;]\n    equal: [cluster]\nreceivers:\n  - name: team-pager\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">route:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">receiver:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">team-pager\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">group_by:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">cluster\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">service\u003c/span>]\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">group_wait:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">30s\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">group_interval:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">5m\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">repeat_interval:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">4h\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">inhibit_rules:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">source_matchers:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">alertname\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">=\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;NodeDown&quot;\u003c/span>]\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">target_matchers:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">severity\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">=\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;warning&quot;\u003c/span>]\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">equal:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">cluster\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">node\u003c/span>]\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">source_matchers:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">alertname\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">=\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;DatabaseDown&quot;\u003c/span>]\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">target_matchers:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">alertname\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">=~\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Service.*Errors&quot;\u003c/span>]\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">equal:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">cluster\u003c/span>]\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">receivers:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">team-pager\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Alerts for the same cluster and service are grouped for 30 seconds before the first page. When a database is down, service error alerts in the same cluster are inhibited so the cause stands out.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-it-fails\">How it fails\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Alerts have no labels, so nothing can be grouped. Silences never expire. Inhibition hides a real second fault. Dependency data is out of date, so the cause shown is wrong. Nobody measures noise.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4;n4&gt;n5;n5&gt;n6\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Alert correlation and noise reduction fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"8.3\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"25.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"25.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"41.7\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"41.7\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"58.3\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"58.3\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"75.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"75.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"91.7\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:8.3%;top:25.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Why storms hurt\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:25.0%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Deduplicate and group\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:41.7%;top:25.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Use topology\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:58.3%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Inhibit and silence by design\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n5\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:75.0%;top:25.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Measure and prune\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n6\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:91.7%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Grouping and inhibition in\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Why storms hurt to Deduplicate and group; 2, Deduplicate and group to Use topology; 3, Use topology to Inhibit and silence by design; 4, Inhibit and silence by design to Measure and prune; 5, Measure and prune to Grouping and inhibition in.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Why storms hurt \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Each extra alert costs attention. When many fire at once, the engineer must read, sort and guess.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No ownership of alert noise.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Deduplicate and group \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Give every alert stable labels: service, environment, team and a fingerprint of the problem.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Alerts without consistent labels.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Use topology \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> If you know that checkout calls payments, and payments calls the database, then a database fault explains alerts above it.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Inhibition rules that hide real faults.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Inhibit and silence by design \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Inhibition rules suppress low-value alerts while a high-value one is active, for example, mute pod alerts when the whole node is down.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Permanent silences.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n5\">\u003cdt>Measure and prune \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Track alerts per incident, the share that led to an action, and alerts that always resolve alone.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n6\">\u003cdt>Grouping and inhibition in \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Alerts for the same cluster and service are grouped for 30 seconds before the first page.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-two-hundred-pages-for-one-disk\">Incident: Two hundred pages for one disk\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A shared storage volume filled up and many services began failing. The on-call engineer received around two hundred alerts in ten minutes and spent the first part of the incident reading them instead of looking at the disk.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"alertctl summary --window 10m\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>alertctl summary --window 10m\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">alerts received: 214\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">distinct fingerprints: 31\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">grouped into incidents: 2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">likely source: storage-vol-7 (disk 100%), dependents: 29\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The output is a simulated illustration. Grouping by service and dependency turns two hundred alerts into two incidents with one named likely source.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"interview-question\">Interview question\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>&quot;How would you reduce alert noise during an outage?&quot; Add stable labels, deduplicate, group by service and cluster, use dependency data to show the likely root, add inhibition rules, and track alerts per incident as a metric.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Alerts without consistent labels\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Permanent silences\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Inhibition rules that hide real faults\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No ownership of alert noise\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"why-storms-hurt","text":"Why storms hurt"},{"id":"deduplicate-and-group","text":"Deduplicate and group"},{"id":"use-topology","text":"Use topology"},{"id":"inhibit-and-silence-by-design","text":"Inhibit and silence by design"},{"id":"measure-and-prune","text":"Measure and prune"},{"id":"grouping-and-inhibition-in-alertmanager","text":"Grouping and inhibition in Alertmanager"},{"id":"how-it-fails","text":"How it fails"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-two-hundred-pages-for-one-disk","text":"Incident: Two hundred pages for one disk"},{"id":"interview-question","text":"Interview question"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Prometheus Alertmanager","u":"https://prometheus.io/docs/alerting/latest/alertmanager/"},{"t":"Google SRE workbook: Alerting on SLOs","u":"https://sre.google/workbook/alerting-on-slos/"}],"text":"During a real outage, one failing database can trigger hundreds of alerts from every service that depends on it. The person on call sees a wall of red and has to work out which alert is the cause and which are echoes. Correlation groups related alerts, and noise reduction removes the ones that never needed a human. Both can be done well with plain rules before adding machine learning. What you will learn Explain alert storms and why they slow response Deduplicate and group alerts using labels and time Use service dependencies to find the likely source Measure noise and track it down over time Why storms hurt Each extra alert costs attention. When many fire at once, the engineer must read, sort and guess. The first alert is often the most useful, and it gets buried. A good system shows one incident with the likely cause on top and the echoes folded underneath. Deduplicate and group Give every alert stable labels: service, environment, team and a fingerprint of the problem. Identical alerts collapse into one with a count. Alerts that share a service or cluster and start within a few minutes group together. This alone often cuts volume by more than half. Use topology If you know that checkout calls payments, and payments calls the database, then a database fault explains alerts above it. Walk the dependency graph from the alerting services toward the shared dependency that is also unhealthy. Surface that one first and label the rest as downstream. Inhibit and silence by design Inhibition rules suppress low-value alerts while a high-value one is active, for example, mute pod alerts when the whole node is down. Planned work should silence alerts with an expiry and a reason. Avoid permanent silences: they hide new problems. Measure and prune Track alerts per incident, the share that led to an action, and alerts that always resolve alone. Retire or retune any alert that is mostly ignored. Noise is a debt that grows quietly, so give someone ownership of the number. Grouping and inhibition in Alertmanager route: receiver: team-pager group_by: [ cluster , service ] group_wait: 30s group_interval: 5m repeat_interval: 4h inhibit_rules: - source_matchers: [ alertname = \"NodeDown\" ] target_matchers: [ severity = \"warning\" ] equal: [ cluster , node ] - source_matchers: [ alertname = \"DatabaseDown\" ] target_matchers: [ alertname =~ \"Service.*Errors\" ] equal: [ cluster ] receivers: - name: team-pager Alerts for the same cluster and service are grouped for 30 seconds before the first page. When a database is down, service error alerts in the same cluster are inhibited so the cause stands out. How it fails Alerts have no labels, so nothing can be grouped. Silences never expire. Inhibition hides a real second fault. Dependency data is out of date, so the cause shown is wrong. Nobody measures noise. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Alert correlation and noise reduction fits together Flow in order: 1, Why storms hurt to Deduplicate and group; 2, Deduplicate and group to Use topology; 3, Use topology to Inhibit and silence by design; 4, Inhibit and silence by design to Measure and prune; 5, Measure and prune to Grouping and inhibition in. Step Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Why storms hurt Step What it does: Each extra alert costs attention. When many fire at once, the engineer must read, sort and guess. If it fails or is weak: No ownership of alert noise. Deduplicate and group Step What it does: Give every alert stable labels: service, environment, team and a fingerprint of the problem. If it fails or is weak: Alerts without consistent labels. Use topology Observability What it does: If you know that checkout calls payments, and payments calls the database, then a database fault explains ale"},"aiops/logs-and-traces-finding-patterns-with-ml":{"html":"\u003cp>Logs are huge and mostly boring. The interesting line is often one you have never seen before. Machine learning and simple statistics can help you find it: group similar lines into templates, count them, and highlight what is new or suddenly frequent. Traces add timing, so slow outliers and changed paths become visible. As always, structured data makes the work easier.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Explain log templates and why clustering them helps\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Detect new and rare messages after a change\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Use trace data to spot slow or unusual paths\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Link patterns to deployments and configuration changes\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"from-lines-to-templates\">From lines to templates\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Many log lines differ only in values: &quot;user 4412 timed out after 3000 ms&quot; and &quot;user 8123 timed out after 2900 ms&quot;. Replace the variable parts with placeholders and you get one template. Counting templates turns millions of lines into a short list you can scan and compare over time.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"new-and-rare-messages\">New and rare messages\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>After a release, list templates that did not exist before, and templates whose rate jumped. New error templates right after a deployment are strong hints. Rare messages at the start of an incident are worth reading first, because they tend to be the first sign of change.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"count-by-version-and-host\">Count by version and host\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Group counts by software version, region and host. If a template appears only on version 2.4.1, you have narrowed the search before reading a single line. This works with plain queries, with no model at all, if your logs carry the version label.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"traces-find-the-slow-path\">Traces find the slow path\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Traces record each step of a request with its duration. Compare the slowest requests with typical ones: which service, call or database query takes the extra time? Look for a changed path, such as a new retry or a call that used to be cached. The span tells you where.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"tie-it-to-change\">Tie it to change\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Overlay deployments, configuration pushes and feature flag changes on your charts. When a pattern starts at the minute of a change, you have a candidate cause. The change log is the most valuable context for any detection method, and it costs very little to keep.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"new-templates-since-the-last-release\">New templates since the last release\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"logstat templates --service checkout --since release:2.4.1 --new\nlogstat compare --version 2.4.0 2.4.1 --top 3\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>logstat templates --service checkout --since release:2.4.1 --new\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">count  template\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">912    payment gateway timeout after &lt;NUM&gt; ms, retry &lt;NUM&gt; of &lt;NUM&gt;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">87     cache miss for key &lt;KEY&gt;, falling back to database\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">3      unexpected status &lt;NUM&gt; from tax service\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>logstat compare --version 2.4.0 2.4.1 --top 3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">payment gateway timeout: 0/min -&gt; 41/min\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The tool lists message templates that first appeared after release 2.4.1. A jump from zero to 41 timeouts a minute narrows the search to one dependency. The output is a simulated illustration.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-it-fails\">How it fails\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Logs have no version or request id, so grouping is impossible. A clustering model merges unrelated messages. Sensitive data in logs gets copied into new systems. Sampling drops the rare line you needed.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4;n4&gt;n5;n5&gt;n6\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Logs and traces: finding patterns with ML fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"8.3\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"25.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"25.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"41.7\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"41.7\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"58.3\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"58.3\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"75.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"75.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"91.7\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:8.3%;top:25.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">From lines to templates\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-queue\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"queue\" style=\"left:25.0%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">New and rare messages\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Queue\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:41.7%;top:25.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Count by version and host\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:58.3%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Traces find the slow path\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n5\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:75.0%;top:25.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Tie it to change\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n6\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:91.7%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">New templates since the last release\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, From lines to templates to New and rare messages; 2, New and rare messages to Count by version and host; 3, Count by version and host to Traces find the slow path; 4, Traces find the slow path to Tie it to change; 5, Tie it to change to New templates since the last release.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-queue\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Queue\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>From lines to templates \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Many log lines differ only in values: &quot;user 4412 timed out after 3000 ms&quot; and &quot;user 8123 timed out after 2900 ms&quot;.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Logs with no version, host or request id.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>New and rare messages \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Queue\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> After a release, list templates that did not exist before, and templates whose rate jumped.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Sampling away rare errors.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Count by version and host \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Group counts by software version, region and host.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Trusting clusters without reading examples.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Traces find the slow path \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Traces record each step of a request with its duration.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n5\">\u003cdt>Tie it to change \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Overlay deployments, configuration pushes and feature flag changes on your charts.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n6\">\u003cdt>New templates since the last release \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The tool lists message templates that first appeared after release 2.4.1.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-error-nobody-had-seen-before\">Incident: The error nobody had seen before\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>After a release, a new error line appeared at a low rate. It was buried among millions of lines and nobody noticed until the rate grew and customers complained. The line had never appeared before the release.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"logstat templates --service orders --since release:5.1.0 --new\nlogstat timeline --template 4f2a --bucket 10m\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>logstat templates --service orders --since release:5.1.0 --new\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">count  template\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">64     serialization failed for field &lt;NAME&gt; in schema v&lt;NUM&gt;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>logstat timeline --template 4f2a --bucket 10m\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">10:00 2   10:10 7   10:20 19   10:30 64\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The output is a simulated illustration. A simple report of templates that are new since a release would have surfaced the line within minutes of the deployment, long before it became a customer problem.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"interview-question\">Interview question\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>&quot;How do you find the needle in millions of log lines?&quot; Group lines into templates, list new and rapidly growing ones after a change, group by version and host, and confirm with traces and the change log.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Logs with no version, host or request id\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Sampling away rare errors\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Sending sensitive data into analysis tools\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Trusting clusters without reading examples\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"from-lines-to-templates","text":"From lines to templates"},{"id":"new-and-rare-messages","text":"New and rare messages"},{"id":"count-by-version-and-host","text":"Count by version and host"},{"id":"traces-find-the-slow-path","text":"Traces find the slow path"},{"id":"tie-it-to-change","text":"Tie it to change"},{"id":"new-templates-since-the-last-release","text":"New templates since the last release"},{"id":"how-it-fails","text":"How it fails"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-error-nobody-had-seen-before","text":"Incident: The error nobody had seen before"},{"id":"interview-question","text":"Interview question"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"OpenTelemetry: traces","u":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/concepts/signals/traces/"},{"t":"OpenTelemetry: logs","u":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/concepts/signals/logs/"}],"text":"Logs are huge and mostly boring. The interesting line is often one you have never seen before. Machine learning and simple statistics can help you find it: group similar lines into templates, count them, and highlight what is new or suddenly frequent. Traces add timing, so slow outliers and changed paths become visible. As always, structured data makes the work easier. What you will learn Explain log templates and why clustering them helps Detect new and rare messages after a change Use trace data to spot slow or unusual paths Link patterns to deployments and configuration changes From lines to templates Many log lines differ only in values: \"user 4412 timed out after 3000 ms\" and \"user 8123 timed out after 2900 ms\". Replace the variable parts with placeholders and you get one template. Counting templates turns millions of lines into a short list you can scan and compare over time. New and rare messages After a release, list templates that did not exist before, and templates whose rate jumped. New error templates right after a deployment are strong hints. Rare messages at the start of an incident are worth reading first, because they tend to be the first sign of change. Count by version and host Group counts by software version, region and host. If a template appears only on version 2.4.1, you have narrowed the search before reading a single line. This works with plain queries, with no model at all, if your logs carry the version label. Traces find the slow path Traces record each step of a request with its duration. Compare the slowest requests with typical ones: which service, call or database query takes the extra time? Look for a changed path, such as a new retry or a call that used to be cached. The span tells you where. Tie it to change Overlay deployments, configuration pushes and feature flag changes on your charts. When a pattern starts at the minute of a change, you have a candidate cause. The change log is the most valuable context for any detection method, and it costs very little to keep. New templates since the last release $ logstat templates --service checkout --since release:2.4.1 --new count template 912 payment gateway timeout after \u003cNUM> ms, retry \u003cNUM> of \u003cNUM> 87 cache miss for key \u003cKEY>, falling back to database 3 unexpected status \u003cNUM> from tax service $ logstat compare --version 2.4.0 2.4.1 --top 3 payment gateway timeout: 0/min -> 41/min The tool lists message templates that first appeared after release 2.4.1. A jump from zero to 41 timeouts a minute narrows the search to one dependency. The output is a simulated illustration. How it fails Logs have no version or request id, so grouping is impossible. A clustering model merges unrelated messages. Sensitive data in logs gets copied into new systems. Sampling drops the rare line you needed. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Logs and traces: finding patterns with ML fits together Flow in order: 1, From lines to templates to New and rare messages; 2, New and rare messages to Count by version and host; 3, Count by version and host to Traces find the slow path; 4, Traces find the slow path to Tie it to change; 5, Tie it to change to New templates since the last release. Step Queue Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. From lines to templates Step What it does: Many log lines differ only in values: \"user 4412 timed out after 3000 ms\" and \"user 8123 timed out after 2900 ms\". If it fails or is weak: Logs with no version, host or request id. New and rare messages Queue What it does: After a release, list templates that did not exist before, and templates whose rate jumped. If it fails or is weak: Sampling away rare errors. Count by version and host Step What it does: Group counts by software version, region and host. If it f"},"aiops/root-cause-hints-and-incident-assistants":{"html":"\u003cp>During an incident the hardest question is what changed. Tools can speed the search by lining up recent changes, dependency health and anomalies, then ranking suggestions. Language models can add readable summaries and runbook search. The risk is false confidence: a plausible explanation that is wrong can send the team down the wrong path. This lesson shows how to use assistants as a fast second pair of eyes with evidence attached.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Rank candidate causes from change events, timing and dependencies\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Use language models for summaries and search with guardrails\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Require evidence and a confidence reason for each hint\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keep a human decision at every step that changes production\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"start-from-change\">Start from change\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Most incidents follow a change: a deployment, a configuration push, a flag, a certificate expiry or a traffic shift. List every change in the window before the first symptom, ordered by closeness in time and by relation to the failing service. Even without any model, this list is very effective.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"add-dependency-health\">Add dependency health\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Overlay the dependency graph. A service whose dependency started failing a moment earlier is a better suspect than one with no relation. Combine closeness in time, closeness in the graph and the size of the anomaly into a simple score, and show the reasons next to the rank.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"language-models-as-summarisers\">Language models as summarisers\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A model can read the timeline, alerts and chat and write a clear status update, or find the matching runbook. It is good at language, and weaker at being right. Give it the evidence, ask it to quote sources, and show the quotes. Never let it invent a cause from nothing.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"guardrails\">Guardrails\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Keep incident data private: send only what the policy allows. Label every model output as a suggestion. Log what was asked and answered for later review. Do not give the model write access to production. If it suggests an action, a person approves and runs it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"learn-from-outcomes\">Learn from outcomes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>After each incident, record the real cause and whether the hint was right, missed or misleading. Measure how often the top three hints contained the cause. That number tells you whether to trust the tool more, tune it or turn it off.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-hint-report-with-evidence\">A hint report with evidence\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"incident hints --service checkout --since 14:02\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>incident hints --service checkout --since 14:02\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">rank score  hint\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">1    0.82   deploy payments 3.9.0 at 13:58 (4 min before; payments is a dependency)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">2    0.55   config change: payment_timeout_ms 3000 -&gt; 800 at 13:57\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">3    0.21   certificate renewal for api.example.com at 12:10\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">evidence for 1: errors on payments rose 13:59, checkout errors followed 14:02\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">note: hints are suggestions, confirm before acting\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Each hint shows its time gap, relation and evidence, so an engineer can confirm or reject it quickly. The output is a simulated illustration.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-it-fails\">How it fails\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A model states a cause with no evidence. Private data is pasted into an outside service. The assistant is allowed to run commands. The team stops thinking and follows hints. Nobody records whether the hints were right.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4;n4&gt;n5;n5&gt;n6\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Root cause hints and incident assistants fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"8.3\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"25.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"25.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"41.7\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"41.7\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"58.3\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"58.3\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"75.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"75.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"91.7\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:8.3%;top:25.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Start from change\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:25.0%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Add dependency health\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:41.7%;top:25.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Language models as summarisers\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:58.3%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Guardrails\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n5\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:75.0%;top:25.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Learn from outcomes\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n6\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:91.7%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">A hint report with evidence\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Start from change to Add dependency health; 2, Add dependency health to Language models as summarisers; 3, Language models as summarisers to Guardrails; 4, Guardrails to Learn from outcomes; 5, Learn from outcomes to A hint report with evidence.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Start from change \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Most incidents follow a change: a deployment, a configuration push, a flag, a certificate expiry or a traffic shift.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Trusting a hint without checking evidence.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Add dependency health \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Overlay the dependency graph. A service whose dependency started failing a moment earlier is a better suspect than one with no relation.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Pasting private data into tools without a policy.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Language models as summarisers \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A model can read the timeline, alerts and chat and write a clear status update, or find the matching runbook.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Giving an assistant write access to production.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Guardrails \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Keep incident data private: send only what the policy allows.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Do not give the model write access to production.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n5\">\u003cdt>Learn from outcomes \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> After each incident, record the real cause and whether the hint was right, missed or misleading.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Never scoring hints against real causes.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n6\">\u003cdt>A hint report with evidence \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Each hint shows its time gap, relation and evidence, so an engineer can confirm or reject it quickly.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-confident-wrong-answer\">Incident: The confident wrong answer\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>During an outage an assistant suggested that a database was the cause. The team spent twenty minutes on the database. The real cause was a configuration change on a load balancer, which had been in the change list but ranked low.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"incident hints --service shop --since 09:40\nincident timeline --service shop\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>incident hints --service shop --since 09:40\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">1  0.74  database latency up at 09:41\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">2  0.39  load balancer config push at 09:39\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>incident timeline --service shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">09:39 lb config push   09:41 db latency up (caused by retry storm)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The output is a simulated illustration. The ranking looked at symptoms, not at order of events. Showing the timeline next to the hints let the team see that the database was a result, not the cause.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"interview-question\">Interview question\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>&quot;Would you let an AI assistant run incident response?&quot; It can summarise, search runbooks and rank hints with evidence, but people decide and approve any change to production, and every output is a suggestion to verify.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Trusting a hint without checking evidence\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Pasting private data into tools without a policy\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Giving an assistant write access to production\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Never scoring hints against real causes\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"start-from-change","text":"Start from change"},{"id":"add-dependency-health","text":"Add dependency health"},{"id":"language-models-as-summarisers","text":"Language models as summarisers"},{"id":"guardrails","text":"Guardrails"},{"id":"learn-from-outcomes","text":"Learn from outcomes"},{"id":"a-hint-report-with-evidence","text":"A hint report with evidence"},{"id":"how-it-fails","text":"How it fails"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-confident-wrong-answer","text":"Incident: The confident wrong answer"},{"id":"interview-question","text":"Interview question"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Google SRE workbook: Incident response","u":"https://sre.google/workbook/incident-response/"},{"t":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","u":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"}],"text":"During an incident the hardest question is what changed. Tools can speed the search by lining up recent changes, dependency health and anomalies, then ranking suggestions. Language models can add readable summaries and runbook search. The risk is false confidence: a plausible explanation that is wrong can send the team down the wrong path. This lesson shows how to use assistants as a fast second pair of eyes with evidence attached. What you will learn Rank candidate causes from change events, timing and dependencies Use language models for summaries and search with guardrails Require evidence and a confidence reason for each hint Keep a human decision at every step that changes production Start from change Most incidents follow a change: a deployment, a configuration push, a flag, a certificate expiry or a traffic shift. List every change in the window before the first symptom, ordered by closeness in time and by relation to the failing service. Even without any model, this list is very effective. Add dependency health Overlay the dependency graph. A service whose dependency started failing a moment earlier is a better suspect than one with no relation. Combine closeness in time, closeness in the graph and the size of the anomaly into a simple score, and show the reasons next to the rank. Language models as summarisers A model can read the timeline, alerts and chat and write a clear status update, or find the matching runbook. It is good at language, and weaker at being right. Give it the evidence, ask it to quote sources, and show the quotes. Never let it invent a cause from nothing. Guardrails Keep incident data private: send only what the policy allows. Label every model output as a suggestion. Log what was asked and answered for later review. Do not give the model write access to production. If it suggests an action, a person approves and runs it. Learn from outcomes After each incident, record the real cause and whether the hint was right, missed or misleading. Measure how often the top three hints contained the cause. That number tells you whether to trust the tool more, tune it or turn it off. A hint report with evidence $ incident hints --service checkout --since 14:02 rank score hint 1 0.82 deploy payments 3.9.0 at 13:58 (4 min before; payments is a dependency) 2 0.55 config change: payment_timeout_ms 3000 -> 800 at 13:57 3 0.21 certificate renewal for api.example.com at 12:10 evidence for 1: errors on payments rose 13:59, checkout errors followed 14:02 note: hints are suggestions, confirm before acting Each hint shows its time gap, relation and evidence, so an engineer can confirm or reject it quickly. The output is a simulated illustration. How it fails A model states a cause with no evidence. Private data is pasted into an outside service. The assistant is allowed to run commands. The team stops thinking and follows hints. Nobody records whether the hints were right. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Root cause hints and incident assistants fits together Flow in order: 1, Start from change to Add dependency health; 2, Add dependency health to Language models as summarisers; 3, Language models as summarisers to Guardrails; 4, Guardrails to Learn from outcomes; 5, Learn from outcomes to A hint report with evidence. Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Start from change Step What it does: Most incidents follow a change: a deployment, a configuration push, a flag, a certificate expiry or a traffic shift. If it fails or is weak: Trusting a hint without checking evidence. Add dependency health Step What it does: Overlay the dependency graph. A service whose dependency started failing a moment earlier is a better suspect than one with no relation. If it fails or is weak: Pasting p"},"aiops/safe-automation-and-auto-remediation":{"html":"\u003cp>Many incidents end with the same few commands: restart a service, clear a disk, scale up, fail over. Automating them saves time and sleep. But automation acts fast and at scale, so a wrong rule can make an incident bigger in seconds. This lesson covers how to start small, add limits and keep humans in control until the evidence says otherwise.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Choose which fixes are safe to automate\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Add limits, cooldowns and approvals\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Design for rollback and visibility\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Measure whether automation helps\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"pick-the-right-candidates\">Pick the right candidates\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Good candidates are frequent, well understood, low risk and easy to verify: restarting a known stuck worker, rotating a log, clearing a cache. Poor candidates are rare, high impact or poorly understood, such as database failover or deleting data. Start from your runbooks and incident history.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"verify-before-you-act\">Verify before you act\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Before the action, check the condition again: is the alert still firing, is the target the right one, is a change in progress? After the action, check that the problem is solved. If it is not, stop and escalate to a person instead of trying again and again.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"limits-that-contain-mistakes\">Limits that contain mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Set a maximum number of actions per hour, a cooldown between actions, a cap on how many targets at once and a blast radius rule such as never more than ten percent of a fleet. A global kill switch stops every automation in one step.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"approval-levels\">Approval levels\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Introduce automation in stages: suggest the action, then run it with one-click approval, then run it unattended for the safest cases. Move up a level only after a run of correct decisions, and always keep the audit log.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"visibility-and-rollback\">Visibility and rollback\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Every automated action writes who or what did it, why, what it changed and how to undo it, in the incident channel and the audit log. Prefer actions that can be reversed. For those that cannot, require approval.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-remediation-rule-with-limits\">A remediation rule with limits\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"name: restart-stuck-worker\ntrigger:\n  alert: WorkerQueueStalled\n  for: 10m\npreconditions:\n  - alert_still_firing: true\n  - no_active_deploy: true\n  - target_count_max: 2\naction:\n  run: kubectl rollout restart deployment/worker -n jobs\nlimits:\n  max_per_hour: 2\n  cooldown: 30m\n  kill_switch: automation/global\nverify:\n  after: 5m\n  expect: queue_depth &lt; 100\non_verify_fail: page_human\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">restart-stuck-worker\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">trigger:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">alert:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">WorkerQueueStalled\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">for:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">10m\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">preconditions:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">alert_still_firing:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">no_active_deploy:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">target_count_max:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">action:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">run:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">kubectl\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">rollout\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">restart\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">deployment/worker\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">-n\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">jobs\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">limits:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">max_per_hour:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">cooldown:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">30m\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kill_switch:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">automation/global\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">verify:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">after:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">5m\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">expect:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">queue_depth\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&lt;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">100\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">on_verify_fail:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">page_human\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The rule only acts if the alert is still active and no deployment is running. It is limited to two restarts an hour and escalates to a person if the queue does not recover.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-it-fails\">How it fails\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A rule has no cooldown and loops. It acts on the wrong environment. It hides a recurring problem because it always fixes the symptom. A kill switch does not exist. Nobody reviews what the automation did.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4;n4&gt;n5;n5&gt;n6\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Safe automation and auto-remediation fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"8.3\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"25.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"25.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"41.7\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"41.7\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"58.3\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"58.3\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"75.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"75.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"91.7\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:8.3%;top:25.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Pick the right candidates\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:25.0%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Verify before you act\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:41.7%;top:25.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Limits that contain mistakes\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:58.3%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Approval levels\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n5\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:75.0%;top:25.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Visibility and rollback\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n6\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:91.7%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">A remediation rule with limits\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Pick the right candidates to Verify before you act; 2, Verify before you act to Limits that contain mistakes; 3, Limits that contain mistakes to Approval levels; 4, Approval levels to Visibility and rollback; 5, Visibility and rollback to A remediation rule with limits.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Pick the right candidates \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Good candidates are frequent, well understood, low risk and easy to verify: restarting a known stuck worker, rotating a log, clearing a cache.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Automating rare, risky actions first.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Verify before you act \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Before the action, check the condition again: is the alert still firing, is the target the right one, is a change in progress?\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No verification after the action.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Limits that contain mistakes \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Set a maximum number of actions per hour, a cooldown between actions, a cap on how many targets at once and a blast radius rule such as never more than ten percent of a...\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No cooldown or action limits.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Approval levels \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Introduce automation in stages: suggest the action, then run it with one-click approval, then run it unattended for the safest cases.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n5\">\u003cdt>Visibility and rollback \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Every automated action writes who or what did it, why, what it changed and how to undo it, in the incident channel and the audit log.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> For those that cannot, require approval.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n6\">\u003cdt>A remediation rule with limits \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The rule only acts if the alert is still active and no deployment is running.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-cleanup-that-deleted-too-much\">Incident: The cleanup that deleted too much\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>An automation was meant to delete old temporary files when a disk filled. A path variable was empty, so the command ran against a much larger directory, and it deleted data that other services needed.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"remediate run cleanup-tmp --dry-run\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>remediate run cleanup-tmp --dry-run\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">target path: &quot;&quot;   (EMPTY)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">resolved delete scope: /var/data/*\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">result: ABORT, target path empty\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The output is a simulated illustration. A dry run, a check that the target is not empty and a rule that deletes only inside a named directory would have stopped the action before it did any harm.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"interview-question\">Interview question\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>&quot;What would you automate first on-call?&quot; Frequent, low-risk, easily verified fixes, with preconditions, limits, a verify step, an audit log, a kill switch and a path from suggestion to approval to unattended mode.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Automating rare, risky actions first\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No cooldown or action limits\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No verification after the action\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Hiding recurring faults instead of fixing them\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"pick-the-right-candidates","text":"Pick the right candidates"},{"id":"verify-before-you-act","text":"Verify before you act"},{"id":"limits-that-contain-mistakes","text":"Limits that contain mistakes"},{"id":"approval-levels","text":"Approval levels"},{"id":"visibility-and-rollback","text":"Visibility and rollback"},{"id":"a-remediation-rule-with-limits","text":"A remediation rule with limits"},{"id":"how-it-fails","text":"How it fails"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-cleanup-that-deleted-too-much","text":"Incident: The cleanup that deleted too much"},{"id":"interview-question","text":"Interview question"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Google SRE book: The evolution of automation","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/automation-at-google/"},{"t":"Google SRE workbook: Incident response","u":"https://sre.google/workbook/incident-response/"}],"text":"Many incidents end with the same few commands: restart a service, clear a disk, scale up, fail over. Automating them saves time and sleep. But automation acts fast and at scale, so a wrong rule can make an incident bigger in seconds. This lesson covers how to start small, add limits and keep humans in control until the evidence says otherwise. What you will learn Choose which fixes are safe to automate Add limits, cooldowns and approvals Design for rollback and visibility Measure whether automation helps Pick the right candidates Good candidates are frequent, well understood, low risk and easy to verify: restarting a known stuck worker, rotating a log, clearing a cache. Poor candidates are rare, high impact or poorly understood, such as database failover or deleting data. Start from your runbooks and incident history. Verify before you act Before the action, check the condition again: is the alert still firing, is the target the right one, is a change in progress? After the action, check that the problem is solved. If it is not, stop and escalate to a person instead of trying again and again. Limits that contain mistakes Set a maximum number of actions per hour, a cooldown between actions, a cap on how many targets at once and a blast radius rule such as never more than ten percent of a fleet. A global kill switch stops every automation in one step. Approval levels Introduce automation in stages: suggest the action, then run it with one-click approval, then run it unattended for the safest cases. Move up a level only after a run of correct decisions, and always keep the audit log. Visibility and rollback Every automated action writes who or what did it, why, what it changed and how to undo it, in the incident channel and the audit log. Prefer actions that can be reversed. For those that cannot, require approval. A remediation rule with limits name: restart-stuck-worker trigger: alert: WorkerQueueStalled for: 10m preconditions: - alert_still_firing: true - no_active_deploy: true - target_count_max: 2 action: run: kubectl rollout restart deployment/worker -n jobs limits: max_per_hour: 2 cooldown: 30m kill_switch: automation/global verify: after: 5m expect: queue_depth \u003c 100 on_verify_fail: page_human The rule only acts if the alert is still active and no deployment is running. It is limited to two restarts an hour and escalates to a person if the queue does not recover. How it fails A rule has no cooldown and loops. It acts on the wrong environment. It hides a recurring problem because it always fixes the symptom. A kill switch does not exist. Nobody reviews what the automation did. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Safe automation and auto-remediation fits together Flow in order: 1, Pick the right candidates to Verify before you act; 2, Verify before you act to Limits that contain mistakes; 3, Limits that contain mistakes to Approval levels; 4, Approval levels to Visibility and rollback; 5, Visibility and rollback to A remediation rule with limits. Step Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Pick the right candidates Step What it does: Good candidates are frequent, well understood, low risk and easy to verify: restarting a known stuck worker, rotating a log, clearing a cache. If it fails or is weak: Automating rare, risky actions first. Verify before you act Observability What it does: Before the action, check the condition again: is the alert still firing, is the target the right one, is a change in progress? If it fails or is weak: No verification after the action. Limits that contain mistakes Step What it does: Set a maximum number of actions per hour, a cooldown between actions, a cap on how many targets at once and a blast radius rule such as never more than ten percent of a"},"aiops/debugging-and-investigating-aiops-output":{"html":"\u003cp>An AIOps tool can summarise alerts, suggest causes and propose fixes. Its answer is a hypothesis. Treat it the way you would treat a new colleague&#39;s guess: useful, worth checking, and not yet a fact.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Check an AI summary against the source evidence\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Spot a confident answer that has no support\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Compare a suggested cause with a timeline of changes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Record misses so the tool and its limits improve\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"ask-for-the-evidence\">Ask for the evidence\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Every claim in a summary should point to something: a log line, a metric, a change record. If it does not, ask for the source. A claim with no source is a guess that happens to be well written.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"test-the-claim\">Test the claim\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Turn the suggested cause into a question you can check in the data. If it says a deploy caused the errors, compare the error rate before and after the deploy time. If the data disagrees, the suggestion is wrong, however fluent.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"compare-with-the-timeline\">Compare with the timeline\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Line up alerts, deploys, config changes and traffic on one timeline. A cause must come before its effects. Suggestions that name something that happened later, or in a different service, are suspect.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"watch-the-false-positives-and-misses\">Watch the false positives and misses\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Count how often a suggestion was right, wrong, or missing. Keep the list. It tells you which alerts to trust, and it is the evidence for tuning or turning a feature off.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"keep-a-person-on-the-action\">Keep a person on the action\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Reading is cheap and reversible. Restarting, scaling or rolling back is not. A person approves each action, with the evidence in front of them, and the tool logs who approved it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> An assistant says a recent deploy caused a spike, and offers to roll it back. What do you check before agreeing?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> That the spike started after the deploy time, that the evidence it cites exists, and what the rollback would affect.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The suggestion is a hypothesis; check the timeline and the evidence first.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"checking-a-claim-against-the-data\">Checking a claim against the data\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"promtool query instant http://prometheus:9090 'sum(rate(http_requests_total{code=~&quot;5..&quot;}[5m]))'\ngit log --since=&quot;2 hours ago&quot; --oneline\nkubectl rollout history deployment/checkout\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>promtool query instant http://prometheus:9090 \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;sum(rate(http_requests_total{code=~&quot;5..&quot;}[5m]))&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">5xx rate now: 0.2 per second\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>git \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">log\u003c/span> --since=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;2 hours ago&quot;\u003c/span> --oneline\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">a41f2c2 reduce cache size\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl rollout \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">history\u003c/span> deployment/checkout\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">REVISION  CHANGE-CAUSE\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">41        reduce cache size\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The assistant blamed a deploy, but the error rate is low and the only recent change is a cache setting. The first look does not support rolling back. The output is a simulated illustration.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-it-fails\">How it fails\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The summary is accepted because it reads well. The suggested action is run with no check. Wrong suggestions are not recorded, so the same error repeats. The tool is given write access too early.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4;n4&gt;n5;n5&gt;n6\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Debugging and investigating AIOps output fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"8.3\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"25.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"25.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"41.7\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"41.7\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"58.3\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"58.3\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"75.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"75.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"91.7\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:8.3%;top:25.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Ask for the evidence\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:25.0%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Test the claim\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:41.7%;top:25.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Compare with the timeline\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:58.3%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Watch the false positives and misses\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n5\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:75.0%;top:25.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Keep a person on the action\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n6\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:91.7%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Checking a claim against the data\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Ask for the evidence to Test the claim; 2, Test the claim to Compare with the timeline; 3, Compare with the timeline to Watch the false positives and misses; 4, Watch the false positives and misses to Keep a person on the action; 5, Keep a person on the action to Checking a claim against the data.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Ask for the evidence \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Every claim in a summary should point to something: a log line, a metric, a change record.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Accepting a fluent summary without evidence.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Test the claim \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Turn the suggested cause into a question you can check in the data.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Running a suggested action with no check.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Compare with the timeline \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Line up alerts, deploys, config changes and traffic on one timeline.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Not recording wrong suggestions.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Watch the false positives and misses \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Count how often a suggestion was right, wrong, or missing.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n5\">\u003cdt>Keep a person on the action \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Reading is cheap and reversible. Restarting, scaling or rolling back is not.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Giving the tool write access before it is trusted.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n6\">\u003cdt>Checking a claim against the data \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The assistant blamed a deploy, but the error rate is low and the only recent change is a cache setting.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-confident-rollback\">Incident: The confident rollback\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>During a slow-checkout alert an assistant named a deploy as the cause. The on-call engineer rolled back without checking. The real cause was a database lock, and the rollback added ten minutes of disruption.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"promtool query instant http://prometheus:9090 'max(pg_locks_waiting)'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>promtool query instant http://prometheus:9090 \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;max(pg_locks_waiting)&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">pg_locks_waiting 41\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The output is a simulated illustration. Testing the claim against the metrics and the timeline would have shown the locks and avoided a rollback that was never needed.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"interview-question\">Interview question\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>&quot;Would you let an AIOps tool fix incidents by itself?&quot; Not at first. I would let it read and suggest, check each claim against evidence, keep a person approving every action, record hits and misses, and widen its scope only where it has earned trust.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Accepting a fluent summary without evidence\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running a suggested action with no check\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not recording wrong suggestions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Giving the tool write access before it is trusted\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"ask-for-the-evidence","text":"Ask for the evidence"},{"id":"test-the-claim","text":"Test the claim"},{"id":"compare-with-the-timeline","text":"Compare with the timeline"},{"id":"watch-the-false-positives-and-misses","text":"Watch the false positives and misses"},{"id":"keep-a-person-on-the-action","text":"Keep a person on the action"},{"id":"checking-a-claim-against-the-data","text":"Checking a claim against the data"},{"id":"how-it-fails","text":"How it fails"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-confident-rollback","text":"Incident: The confident rollback"},{"id":"interview-question","text":"Interview question"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Google SRE workbook: Incident response","u":"https://sre.google/workbook/incident-response/"},{"t":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","u":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"},{"t":"Prometheus: querying basics","u":"https://prometheus.io/docs/prometheus/latest/querying/basics/"}],"text":"An AIOps tool can summarise alerts, suggest causes and propose fixes. Its answer is a hypothesis. Treat it the way you would treat a new colleague&#39;s guess: useful, worth checking, and not yet a fact. What you will learn Check an AI summary against the source evidence Spot a confident answer that has no support Compare a suggested cause with a timeline of changes Record misses so the tool and its limits improve Ask for the evidence Every claim in a summary should point to something: a log line, a metric, a change record. If it does not, ask for the source. A claim with no source is a guess that happens to be well written. Test the claim Turn the suggested cause into a question you can check in the data. If it says a deploy caused the errors, compare the error rate before and after the deploy time. If the data disagrees, the suggestion is wrong, however fluent. Compare with the timeline Line up alerts, deploys, config changes and traffic on one timeline. A cause must come before its effects. Suggestions that name something that happened later, or in a different service, are suspect. Watch the false positives and misses Count how often a suggestion was right, wrong, or missing. Keep the list. It tells you which alerts to trust, and it is the evidence for tuning or turning a feature off. Keep a person on the action Reading is cheap and reversible. Restarting, scaling or rolling back is not. A person approves each action, with the evidence in front of them, and the tool logs who approved it. Predict first. An assistant says a recent deploy caused a spike, and offers to roll it back. What do you check before agreeing? Your guess Answer: That the spike started after the deploy time, that the evidence it cites exists, and what the rollback would affect. The suggestion is a hypothesis; check the timeline and the evidence first. Checking a claim against the data $ promtool query instant http://prometheus:9090 &#x27;sum(rate(http_requests_total{code=~\"5..\"}[5m]))&#x27; 5xx rate now: 0.2 per second $ git log --since= \"2 hours ago\" --oneline a41f2c2 reduce cache size $ kubectl rollout history deployment/checkout REVISION CHANGE-CAUSE 41 reduce cache size The assistant blamed a deploy, but the error rate is low and the only recent change is a cache setting. The first look does not support rolling back. The output is a simulated illustration. How it fails The summary is accepted because it reads well. The suggested action is run with no check. Wrong suggestions are not recorded, so the same error repeats. The tool is given write access too early. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Debugging and investigating AIOps output fits together Flow in order: 1, Ask for the evidence to Test the claim; 2, Test the claim to Compare with the timeline; 3, Compare with the timeline to Watch the false positives and misses; 4, Watch the false positives and misses to Keep a person on the action; 5, Keep a person on the action to Checking a claim against the data. Step Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Ask for the evidence Step What it does: Every claim in a summary should point to something: a log line, a metric, a change record. If it fails or is weak: Accepting a fluent summary without evidence. Test the claim Observability What it does: Turn the suggested cause into a question you can check in the data. If it fails or is weak: Running a suggested action with no check. Compare with the timeline Step What it does: Line up alerts, deploys, config changes and traffic on one timeline. If it fails or is weak: Not recording wrong suggestions. Watch the false positives and misses Step What it does: Count how often a suggestion was right, wrong, or missing. If it fails or is weak: Check the Common mistakes li"},"aiops/telemetry-quality-for-aiops":{"html":"\u003cp>Machine learning finds patterns in what you give it. If service names differ between tools, timestamps drift or half the metrics are missing, the result is confident nonsense. The first AIOps project is usually a data project.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Naming and labeling conventions that join data together\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Time, clocks and missing data\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A service catalog as the common key\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Measuring data quality\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"consistent-names\">Consistent names\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Use one name per service across metrics, logs, traces and tickets, with standard labels such as \u003ccode>service\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>env\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>team\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>version\u003c/code>. Follow common conventions rather than inventing your own.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# OpenTelemetry resource attributes used everywhere\nservice.name: checkout\nservice.version: 2.8.1\ndeployment.environment: prod\nteam: payments\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># OpenTelemetry resource attributes used everywhere\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">service.name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">checkout\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">service.version:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2.8\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">.1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">deployment.environment:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">prod\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">team:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">payments\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"time-and-gaps\">Time and gaps\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Correlating events needs clocks that agree. Use NTP or chrony, store timestamps in UTC and check skew. Gaps matter too: a metric that stops reporting looks like zero or like an anomaly, depending on the tool.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"promql\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# which scrape targets stopped reporting\nup == 0\n# metrics with too many label combinations\ntopk(10, count by (__name__) ({__name__=~&quot;.+&quot;}))\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># which scrape targets stopped reporting\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">up\u003c/span> == \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># metrics with too many label combinations\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">topk\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">count\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">by\u003c/span> (__name__) ({\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">__name__\u003c/span>=~\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;.+&quot;\u003c/span>}))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"catalog-and-ownership\">Catalog and ownership\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>service catalog\u003c/strong> lists every service with owner, tier, dependencies and runbook. Telemetry and incident tools use it to join signals and to route alerts. Without it, machine learning cannot tell which team to notify.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"measure-quality\">Measure quality\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Track the share of services with complete labels, the number of unnamed or duplicate services, scrape success rate and log parsing success. Treat falling data quality like any other incident source, and review it monthly.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Telemetry quality: the data AIOps depends on fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Consistent names\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Time and gaps\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Catalog and ownership\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Measure quality\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Consistent names to Time and gaps; 2, Time and gaps to Catalog and ownership; 3, Catalog and ownership to Measure quality.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Consistent names \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Use one name per service across metrics, logs, traces and tickets, with standard labels such as service, env, team and version.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Different names for one service.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Time and gaps \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Correlating events needs clocks that agree.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Clock skew between hosts.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Catalog and ownership \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A service catalog lists every service with owner, tier, dependencies and runbook.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Buying AIOps tools before fixing data.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Measure quality \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Track the share of services with complete labels, the number of unnamed or duplicate services, scrape success rate and log parsing success.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No owner metadata on services.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-five-names-for-one-service\">Incident: Five names for one service\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>The same service appeared under different names in metrics, logs and traces.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">metrics:   service=&quot;checkout-svc&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">logs:      app=&quot;Checkout&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">traces:    service.name=&quot;checkout_api&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">incidents: component=&quot;payments-checkout&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Correlation produced nothing because the data would not join. The team created a catalog with one canonical name, mapped old names for a transition period and enforced labels in the collection pipeline.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Buying AIOps tools before fixing data\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Different names for one service\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Clock skew between hosts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No owner metadata on services\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"consistent-names","text":"Consistent names"},{"id":"time-and-gaps","text":"Time and gaps"},{"id":"catalog-and-ownership","text":"Catalog and ownership"},{"id":"measure-quality","text":"Measure quality"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-five-names-for-one-service","text":"Incident: Five names for one service"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"OpenTelemetry semantic conventions","u":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/semconv/"},{"t":"Prometheus naming practices","u":"https://prometheus.io/docs/practices/naming/"}],"text":"Machine learning finds patterns in what you give it. If service names differ between tools, timestamps drift or half the metrics are missing, the result is confident nonsense. The first AIOps project is usually a data project. What you will learn Naming and labeling conventions that join data together Time, clocks and missing data A service catalog as the common key Measuring data quality Consistent names Use one name per service across metrics, logs, traces and tickets, with standard labels such as service , env , team and version . Follow common conventions rather than inventing your own. # OpenTelemetry resource attributes used everywhere service.name: checkout service.version: 2.8 .1 deployment.environment: prod team: payments Time and gaps Correlating events needs clocks that agree. Use NTP or chrony, store timestamps in UTC and check skew. Gaps matter too: a metric that stops reporting looks like zero or like an anomaly, depending on the tool. # which scrape targets stopped reporting up == 0 # metrics with too many label combinations topk ( 10 , count by (__name__) ({ __name__ =~ \".+\" })) Catalog and ownership A service catalog lists every service with owner, tier, dependencies and runbook. Telemetry and incident tools use it to join signals and to route alerts. Without it, machine learning cannot tell which team to notify. Measure quality Track the share of services with complete labels, the number of unnamed or duplicate services, scrape success rate and log parsing success. Treat falling data quality like any other incident source, and review it monthly. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Telemetry quality: the data AIOps depends on fits together Flow in order: 1, Consistent names to Time and gaps; 2, Time and gaps to Catalog and ownership; 3, Catalog and ownership to Measure quality. Step Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Consistent names Step What it does: Use one name per service across metrics, logs, traces and tickets, with standard labels such as service, env, team and version. If it fails or is weak: Different names for one service. Time and gaps Step What it does: Correlating events needs clocks that agree. If it fails or is weak: Clock skew between hosts. Catalog and ownership Observability What it does: A service catalog lists every service with owner, tier, dependencies and runbook. If it fails or is weak: Buying AIOps tools before fixing data. Measure quality Step What it does: Track the share of services with complete labels, the number of unnamed or duplicate services, scrape success rate and log parsing success. If it fails or is weak: No owner metadata on services. Incident: Five names for one service Incident The same service appeared under different names in metrics, logs and traces. metrics: service=\"checkout-svc\" logs: app=\"Checkout\" traces: service.name=\"checkout_api\" incidents: component=\"payments-checkout\" Correlation produced nothing because the data would not join. The team created a catalog with one canonical name, mapped old names for a transition period and enforced labels in the collection pipeline. Common mistakes Buying AIOps tools before fixing data Different names for one service Clock skew between hosts No owner metadata on services"},"aiops/forecasting-capacity-and-saturation":{"html":"\u003cp>Capacity incidents are predictable because resources grow in patterns. A little forecasting turns surprises into planned work, and you do not need machine learning for the first big wins.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Time to full as a better alert than percent used\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Trend and seasonality in operational metrics\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forecasting traffic and events you know about\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Measuring forecast error and avoiding false confidence\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"time-to-full\">Time to full\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"promql\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# will this disk fill within 7 days, based on the last 6 hours of growth?\npredict_linear(node_filesystem_avail_bytes{mountpoint=&quot;/data&quot;}[6h], 7 * 24 * 3600) &lt; 0\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># will this disk fill within 7 days, based on the last 6 hours of growth?\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">predict_linear\u003c/span>(node_filesystem_avail_bytes{\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">mountpoint\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;/data&quot;\u003c/span>}[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">6\u003c/span>h], \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">7\u003c/span> * \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">24\u003c/span> * \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3600\u003c/span>) &lt; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Alerting on \u003ccode>&gt; 85 percent\u003c/code> fires for slow, harmless usage and misses fast growth. Alerting on \u003cstrong>time to full\u003c/strong> adapts to the actual rate.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"trend-and-seasonality\">Trend and seasonality\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Most traffic has daily and weekly cycles. A simple approach is to compare with the same hour last week and to fit a trend on the weekly average.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"import numpy as np\nweeks = np.array([410, 428, 445, 470, 492, 515])      # weekly peak requests/s\nslope, intercept = np.polyfit(range(len(weeks)), weeks, 1)\nprint(&quot;next 8 weeks:&quot;, [round(slope * (len(weeks) + i) + intercept) for i in range(1, 9)])\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> numpy \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">as\u003c/span> np\nweeks = np.array([\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">410\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">428\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">445\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">470\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">492\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">515\u003c/span>])      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># weekly peak requests/s\u003c/span>\nslope, intercept = np.polyfit(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">range\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">len\u003c/span>(weeks)), weeks, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span>)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;next 8 weeks:&quot;\u003c/span>, [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">round\u003c/span>(slope * (\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">len\u003c/span>(weeks) + i) + intercept) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> i \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">range\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">9\u003c/span>)])\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"known-events\">Known events\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Add launches, campaigns and holidays as separate adjustments rather than letting the model treat them as noise. Ask product teams for upcoming events each quarter.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"check-the-forecast\">Check the forecast\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Record each forecast and compare with what happened. If error is large, widen the safety margin or use a different method. Show a range, not one number, and review the main forecasts with the owners. Plan lead times: ordering hardware or raising a cloud quota can take longer than the forecast horizon.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Forecasting capacity and saturation fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Time to full\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Trend and seasonality\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-queue\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"queue\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Known events\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Queue\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Check the forecast\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Time to full to Trend and seasonality; 2, Trend and seasonality to Known events; 3, Known events to Check the forecast.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-queue\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Queue\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Time to full \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Alerting on &gt; 85 percent fires for slow, harmless usage and misses fast growth.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Alerting only on percent used.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Trend and seasonality \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Most traffic has daily and weekly cycles.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Ignoring seasonality and known events.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Known events \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Queue\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Add launches, campaigns and holidays as separate adjustments rather than letting the model treat them as noise.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Forecast horizons shorter than procurement lead time.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Check the forecast \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Record each forecast and compare with what happened.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Forecasts with no error tracking.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-seventy-percent-and-nine-days-left\">Incident: Seventy percent and nine days left\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A disk looked healthy but was growing quickly.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">/data  70% used  (1.4 TB free)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">growth: 38 GB/day over the last 6h\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">predicted full in 9.1 days\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A fixed percentage threshold said everything was fine. The forecast alert warned a week ahead, and the team added retention for a runaway log table before the weekend.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Alerting only on percent used\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring seasonality and known events\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forecasts with no error tracking\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forecast horizons shorter than procurement lead time\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"time-to-full","text":"Time to full"},{"id":"trend-and-seasonality","text":"Trend and seasonality"},{"id":"known-events","text":"Known events"},{"id":"check-the-forecast","text":"Check the forecast"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-seventy-percent-and-nine-days-left","text":"Incident: Seventy percent and nine days left"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Prometheus predict_linear","u":"https://prometheus.io/docs/prometheus/latest/querying/functions/#predict_linear"},{"t":"Google SRE book: capacity planning","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/software-engineering-in-sre/"}],"text":"Capacity incidents are predictable because resources grow in patterns. A little forecasting turns surprises into planned work, and you do not need machine learning for the first big wins. What you will learn Time to full as a better alert than percent used Trend and seasonality in operational metrics Forecasting traffic and events you know about Measuring forecast error and avoiding false confidence Time to full # will this disk fill within 7 days, based on the last 6 hours of growth? predict_linear (node_filesystem_avail_bytes{ mountpoint = \"/data\" }[ 6 h], 7 * 24 * 3600 ) \u003c 0 Alerting on > 85 percent fires for slow, harmless usage and misses fast growth. Alerting on time to full adapts to the actual rate. Trend and seasonality Most traffic has daily and weekly cycles. A simple approach is to compare with the same hour last week and to fit a trend on the weekly average. import numpy as np weeks = np.array([ 410 , 428 , 445 , 470 , 492 , 515 ]) # weekly peak requests/s slope, intercept = np.polyfit( range ( len (weeks)), weeks, 1 ) print ( \"next 8 weeks:\" , [ round (slope * ( len (weeks) + i) + intercept) for i in range ( 1 , 9 )]) Known events Add launches, campaigns and holidays as separate adjustments rather than letting the model treat them as noise. Ask product teams for upcoming events each quarter. Check the forecast Record each forecast and compare with what happened. If error is large, widen the safety margin or use a different method. Show a range, not one number, and review the main forecasts with the owners. Plan lead times: ordering hardware or raising a cloud quota can take longer than the forecast horizon. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Forecasting capacity and saturation fits together Flow in order: 1, Time to full to Trend and seasonality; 2, Trend and seasonality to Known events; 3, Known events to Check the forecast. Step Queue Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Time to full Step What it does: Alerting on > 85 percent fires for slow, harmless usage and misses fast growth. If it fails or is weak: Alerting only on percent used. Trend and seasonality Step What it does: Most traffic has daily and weekly cycles. If it fails or is weak: Ignoring seasonality and known events. Known events Queue What it does: Add launches, campaigns and holidays as separate adjustments rather than letting the model treat them as noise. If it fails or is weak: Forecast horizons shorter than procurement lead time. Check the forecast Observability What it does: Record each forecast and compare with what happened. If it fails or is weak: Forecasts with no error tracking. Incident: Seventy percent and nine days left Incident A disk looked healthy but was growing quickly. /data 70% used (1.4 TB free) growth: 38 GB/day over the last 6h predicted full in 9.1 days A fixed percentage threshold said everything was fine. The forecast alert warned a week ahead, and the team added retention for a runaway log table before the weekend. Common mistakes Alerting only on percent used Ignoring seasonality and known events Forecasts with no error tracking Forecast horizons shorter than procurement lead time"},"aiops/llm-assistants-for-incident-response":{"html":"\u003cp>During an incident, people read too much and write too little. Language models are good at turning a wall of text into a summary, and at drafting updates, which frees humans to think. They are bad at being right without evidence.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Safe uses: summaries, timelines, drafts and search\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Grounding answers in your runbooks and data\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Privacy, redaction and access control\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Human approval and review\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"good-tasks\">Good tasks\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Summarize a long chat or log excerpt into a few lines.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Draft a \u003cstrong>timeline\u003c/strong> from alerts, deploys and messages.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Draft \u003cstrong>stakeholder updates\u003c/strong> for an engineer to edit and send.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Search runbooks and past incidents for similar cases.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Write the first draft of a \u003cstrong>postmortem\u003c/strong> from the timeline.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"ground-the-answers\">Ground the answers\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"def answer(question, search, llm):\n    docs = search(question, top_k=5)          # runbooks, past incidents, service docs\n    context = &quot;\\n\\n&quot;.join(f&quot;[{d.id}] {d.text}&quot; for d in docs)\n    prompt = (\n        &quot;Answer only from the context. Cite ids like [runbook-12]. &quot;\n        &quot;If the answer is not there, say you do not know.\\n\\n&quot;\n        f&quot;Context:\\n{context}\\n\\nQuestion: {question}&quot;\n    )\n    return llm(prompt)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">answer\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">question, search, llm\u003c/span>):\n    docs = search(question, top_k=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">5\u003c/span>)          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># runbooks, past incidents, service docs\u003c/span>\n    context = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\\n\\n&quot;\u003c/span>.join(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">f&quot;[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">{d.\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">id\u003c/span>}\u003c/span>] \u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">{d.text}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> d \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> docs)\n    prompt = (\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Answer only from the context. Cite ids like [runbook-12]. &quot;\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;If the answer is not there, say you do not know.\\n\\n&quot;\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">f&quot;Context:\\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">{context}\u003c/span>\\n\\nQuestion: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">{question}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>\n    )\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> llm(prompt)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Require citations and refuse answers without sources. Show the source text next to the answer so a person can verify in seconds.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"privacy\">Privacy\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Redact secrets, tokens and personal data before sending text to a model, use an approved provider with the right data terms and limit which channels the assistant can read. Keep an audit log of prompts and responses.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"keep-humans-in-charge\">Keep humans in charge\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The assistant \u003cstrong>suggests\u003c/strong>, the engineer \u003cstrong>decides\u003c/strong>. Never let it run commands, change configuration or page people without explicit approval. Measure usefulness with feedback buttons and review wrong answers in the postmortem, since they teach you where grounding is weak.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How LLM assistants for incident response fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Good tasks\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Ground the answers\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Privacy\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Keep humans in charge\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Good tasks to Ground the answers; 2, Ground the answers to Privacy; 3, Privacy to Keep humans in charge.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Good tasks \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Summarize a long chat or log excerpt into a few lines.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Ground the answers \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Require citations and refuse answers without sources.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Answers without cited sources.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Privacy \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Redact secrets, tokens and personal data before sending text to a model, use an approved provider with the right data terms and limit which channels the assistant can...\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Sending secrets or customer data to a model.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Keep humans in charge \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The assistant suggests, the engineer decides.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Not reviewing wrong answers.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-command-that-did-not-exist\">Incident: The command that did not exist\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>An assistant suggested a made-up flag during an outage.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"dbctl failover --force-safe\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>dbctl failover --force-safe\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">error: unknown flag: --force-safe\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">elapsed: 6 minutes lost to the wrong suggestion\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The assistant answered from general knowledge instead of the team&#39;s tools. The engineer noticed, but time was lost. The team connected it to the real runbooks, required citations and made it say it did not know when nothing matched.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Letting a model run commands unsupervised\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Answers without cited sources\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Sending secrets or customer data to a model\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not reviewing wrong answers\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"good-tasks","text":"Good tasks"},{"id":"ground-the-answers","text":"Ground the answers"},{"id":"privacy","text":"Privacy"},{"id":"keep-humans-in-charge","text":"Keep humans in charge"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-command-that-did-not-exist","text":"Incident: The command that did not exist"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Anthropic documentation","u":"https://docs.anthropic.com/"},{"t":"Google SRE workbook: incident response","u":"https://sre.google/workbook/incident-response/"}],"text":"During an incident, people read too much and write too little. Language models are good at turning a wall of text into a summary, and at drafting updates, which frees humans to think. They are bad at being right without evidence. What you will learn Safe uses: summaries, timelines, drafts and search Grounding answers in your runbooks and data Privacy, redaction and access control Human approval and review Good tasks Summarize a long chat or log excerpt into a few lines. Draft a timeline from alerts, deploys and messages. Draft stakeholder updates for an engineer to edit and send. Search runbooks and past incidents for similar cases. Write the first draft of a postmortem from the timeline. Ground the answers def answer ( question, search, llm ): docs = search(question, top_k= 5 ) # runbooks, past incidents, service docs context = \"\\n\\n\" .join( f\"[ {d. id } ] {d.text} \" for d in docs) prompt = ( \"Answer only from the context. Cite ids like [runbook-12]. \" \"If the answer is not there, say you do not know.\\n\\n\" f\"Context:\\n {context} \\n\\nQuestion: {question} \" ) return llm(prompt) Require citations and refuse answers without sources. Show the source text next to the answer so a person can verify in seconds. Privacy Redact secrets, tokens and personal data before sending text to a model, use an approved provider with the right data terms and limit which channels the assistant can read. Keep an audit log of prompts and responses. Keep humans in charge The assistant suggests , the engineer decides . Never let it run commands, change configuration or page people without explicit approval. Measure usefulness with feedback buttons and review wrong answers in the postmortem, since they teach you where grounding is weak. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How LLM assistants for incident response fits together Flow in order: 1, Good tasks to Ground the answers; 2, Ground the answers to Privacy; 3, Privacy to Keep humans in charge. Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Good tasks Step What it does: Summarize a long chat or log excerpt into a few lines. If it fails or is weak: Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here. Ground the answers Step What it does: Require citations and refuse answers without sources. If it fails or is weak: Answers without cited sources. Privacy Step What it does: Redact secrets, tokens and personal data before sending text to a model, use an approved provider with the right data terms and limit which channels the assistant can... If it fails or is weak: Sending secrets or customer data to a model. Keep humans in charge Step What it does: The assistant suggests, the engineer decides. If it fails or is weak: Not reviewing wrong answers. Incident: The command that did not exist Incident An assistant suggested a made-up flag during an outage. $ dbctl failover --force-safe error: unknown flag: --force-safe elapsed: 6 minutes lost to the wrong suggestion The assistant answered from general knowledge instead of the team&#39;s tools. The engineer noticed, but time was lost. The team connected it to the real runbooks, required citations and made it say it did not know when nothing matched. Common mistakes Letting a model run commands unsupervised Answers without cited sources Sending secrets or customer data to a model Not reviewing wrong answers"},"aiops/measuring-aiops-value-and-avoiding-hype":{"html":"\u003cp>Every AIOps product promises less noise and faster recovery. Your job is to test those claims on your own data, with measures that capture both the benefits and the dangers.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Choosing metrics: precision, recall, MTTD and MTTR\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Shadow mode and control groups\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Comparing against a simple baseline\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Counting total cost and risk\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"metrics\">Metrics\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Precision:\u003c/strong> of the alerts it raised, how many were real.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Recall:\u003c/strong> of the real problems, how many it caught.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>MTTD and MTTR:\u003c/strong> mean time to detect and to resolve.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Alert volume and pages per on-call shift.\u003c/strong>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Human effort:\u003c/strong> time spent tuning, labeling and handling its mistakes.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"def precision_recall(tp, fp, fn):\n    return tp / (tp + fp), tp / (tp + fn)\n\nprint(precision_recall(tp=42, fp=8, fn=6))   # (0.84, 0.875)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">precision_recall\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">tp, fp, fn\u003c/span>):\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> tp / (tp + fp), tp / (tp + fn)\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(precision_recall(tp=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">42\u003c/span>, fp=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">8\u003c/span>, fn=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">6\u003c/span>))   \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># (0.84, 0.875)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"run-an-honest-pilot\">Run an honest pilot\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Start in \u003cstrong>shadow mode\u003c/strong>: the tool runs and records what it would do, but humans still handle incidents. Compare its decisions with what really happened over several weeks, including at least one busy period. If possible use a control group, such as one team with the tool and one without.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"baselines\">Baselines\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Compare with a simple rule, such as a static threshold with a sensible window, or a plain deduplication rule. If a simple baseline gets most of the benefit, choose it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"total-cost-and-risk\">Total cost and risk\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Include licences, compute, integration work, tuning time, and the cost of a missed incident. Define a \u003cstrong>rollback\u003c/strong> plan and review results quarterly. Stop or shrink anything that does not show a measurable gain.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Measuring AIOps value and avoiding hype fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Metrics\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Run an honest pilot\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Baselines\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Total cost and risk\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Metrics to Run an honest pilot; 2, Run an honest pilot to Baselines; 3, Baselines to Total cost and risk.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Metrics \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Precision: of the alerts it raised, how many were real.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Judging only by alert reduction.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Run an honest pilot \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Start in shadow mode: the tool runs and records what it would do, but humans still handle incidents.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Skipping a shadow period.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Baselines \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Compare with a simple rule, such as a static threshold with a sensible window, or a plain deduplication rule.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No comparison with a simple rule.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Total cost and risk \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Include licences, compute, integration work, tuning time, and the cost of a missed incident.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Ignoring the work of maintaining the tool.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-ninety-percent-less-noise-two-missed-incidents\">Incident: Ninety percent less noise, two missed incidents\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Shadow mode revealed that suppression hid real incidents.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">alerts before: 1820/week\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">alerts after:  182/week (-90%)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">real incidents suppressed during shadow period: 2\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The headline number counted all noise reduction as good. Looking at missed incidents showed the tool suppressed two real problems because they looked like usual noise. The team used the tool only to group related alerts and kept humans in charge of suppression.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Judging only by alert reduction\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Skipping a shadow period\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No comparison with a simple rule\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring the work of maintaining the tool\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"metrics","text":"Metrics"},{"id":"run-an-honest-pilot","text":"Run an honest pilot"},{"id":"baselines","text":"Baselines"},{"id":"total-cost-and-risk","text":"Total cost and risk"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-ninety-percent-less-noise-two-missed-incidents","text":"Incident: Ninety percent less noise, two missed incidents"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Google SRE book: monitoring distributed systems","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/monitoring-distributed-systems/"},{"t":"Atlassian incident management","u":"https://www.atlassian.com/incident-management"}],"text":"Every AIOps product promises less noise and faster recovery. Your job is to test those claims on your own data, with measures that capture both the benefits and the dangers. What you will learn Choosing metrics: precision, recall, MTTD and MTTR Shadow mode and control groups Comparing against a simple baseline Counting total cost and risk Metrics Precision: of the alerts it raised, how many were real. Recall: of the real problems, how many it caught. MTTD and MTTR: mean time to detect and to resolve. Alert volume and pages per on-call shift. Human effort: time spent tuning, labeling and handling its mistakes. def precision_recall ( tp, fp, fn ): return tp / (tp + fp), tp / (tp + fn) print (precision_recall(tp= 42 , fp= 8 , fn= 6 )) # (0.84, 0.875) Run an honest pilot Start in shadow mode : the tool runs and records what it would do, but humans still handle incidents. Compare its decisions with what really happened over several weeks, including at least one busy period. If possible use a control group, such as one team with the tool and one without. Baselines Compare with a simple rule, such as a static threshold with a sensible window, or a plain deduplication rule. If a simple baseline gets most of the benefit, choose it. Total cost and risk Include licences, compute, integration work, tuning time, and the cost of a missed incident. Define a rollback plan and review results quarterly. Stop or shrink anything that does not show a measurable gain. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Measuring AIOps value and avoiding hype fits together Flow in order: 1, Metrics to Run an honest pilot; 2, Run an honest pilot to Baselines; 3, Baselines to Total cost and risk. Observability Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Metrics Observability What it does: Precision: of the alerts it raised, how many were real. If it fails or is weak: Judging only by alert reduction. Run an honest pilot Step What it does: Start in shadow mode: the tool runs and records what it would do, but humans still handle incidents. If it fails or is weak: Skipping a shadow period. Baselines Step What it does: Compare with a simple rule, such as a static threshold with a sensible window, or a plain deduplication rule. If it fails or is weak: No comparison with a simple rule. Total cost and risk Step What it does: Include licences, compute, integration work, tuning time, and the cost of a missed incident. If it fails or is weak: Ignoring the work of maintaining the tool. Incident: Ninety percent less noise, two missed incidents Incident Shadow mode revealed that suppression hid real incidents. alerts before: 1820/week alerts after: 182/week (-90%) real incidents suppressed during shadow period: 2 The headline number counted all noise reduction as good. Looking at missed incidents showed the tool suppressed two real problems because they looked like usual noise. The team used the tool only to group related alerts and kept humans in charge of suppression. Common mistakes Judging only by alert reduction Skipping a shadow period No comparison with a simple rule Ignoring the work of maintaining the tool"},"aiops/runbook-automation-and-guardrails":{"html":"\u003cp>Runbooks are the natural first automation target because the steps are already known. The danger is acting on bad information, so safe automation is mostly about checks, limits and the ability to undo.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The ladder from manual steps to auto-remediation\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Preconditions and independent signals\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Dry runs, rate limits and circuit breakers\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Logging, rollback and review\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-ladder\">The ladder\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Documented:\u003c/strong> a clear runbook that people follow.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Scripted:\u003c/strong> a tested script a person runs.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Suggested:\u003c/strong> the system proposes the action and a person approves.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Automatic:\u003c/strong> low-risk, well-understood actions run by themselves with limits.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>Only move up when the previous step has run reliably for a while.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-guarded-action\">A guarded action\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"import sys\n\ndef restart_unhealthy(service, state, dry_run=True):\n    if not state.metrics_available():                       # do not act on missing data\n        return &quot;skip: metrics unavailable&quot;\n    unhealthy = state.unhealthy_instances(service)\n    if len(unhealthy) &gt; 0.2 * state.total(service):         # too many: likely a wider problem\n        return &quot;skip: more than 20% unhealthy, page a human&quot;\n    if state.restarts_last_hour(service) &gt;= 3:              # rate limit\n        return &quot;skip: restart limit reached&quot;\n    for i in unhealthy[:1]:                                 # one at a time\n        print(&quot;would restart&quot; if dry_run else &quot;restarting&quot;, i)\n        if not dry_run:\n            state.restart(i)\n    return &quot;ok&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> sys\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">restart_unhealthy\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">service, state, dry_run=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">True\u003c/span>\u003c/span>):\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">not\u003c/span> state.metrics_available():                       \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># do not act on missing data\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;skip: metrics unavailable&quot;\u003c/span>\n    unhealthy = state.unhealthy_instances(service)\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">len\u003c/span>(unhealthy) &gt; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0.2\u003c/span> * state.total(service):         \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># too many: likely a wider problem\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;skip: more than 20% unhealthy, page a human&quot;\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> state.restarts_last_hour(service) &gt;= \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3\u003c/span>:              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># rate limit\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;skip: restart limit reached&quot;\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> i \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> unhealthy[:\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span>]:                                 \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># one at a time\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;would restart&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> dry_run \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">else\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;restarting&quot;\u003c/span>, i)\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">not\u003c/span> dry_run:\n            state.restart(i)\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;ok&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"guardrails\">Guardrails\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Preconditions\u003c/strong> use several independent signals, never one.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Blast radius\u003c/strong> limits: one instance at a time and a maximum per hour.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Circuit breaker:\u003c/strong> stop automation after repeated failures.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Dry run\u003c/strong> mode for testing, and \u003cstrong>audit log\u003c/strong> for every action.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Rollback\u003c/strong> or a clear manual undo step.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Review automated actions weekly and delete the ones nobody trusts.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Runbook automation and guardrails fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">The ladder\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:75.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">A guarded action\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Guardrails\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, The ladder to A guarded action; 2, A guarded action to Guardrails.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>The ladder \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Documented: a clear runbook that people follow.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Skipping dry runs.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>A guarded action \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> .\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No limit on how many things change at once.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Guardrails \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Preconditions use several independent signals, never one.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Automation with no audit log.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-restart-loop\">Incident: The restart loop\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Auto-restart hit a healthy fleet when the metrics system failed.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">metrics: no data for 6 minutes\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">automation: instances unhealthy: 100%\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">restarted 48 of 48 instances\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The script read missing data as failure and restarted everything, causing the outage it was meant to prevent. The team added a data-available check, a 20 percent limit, a one-at-a-time rule and a circuit breaker that pages a human.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Acting on a single signal\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No limit on how many things change at once\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Skipping dry runs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Automation with no audit log\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-ladder","text":"The ladder"},{"id":"a-guarded-action","text":"A guarded action"},{"id":"guardrails","text":"Guardrails"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-restart-loop","text":"Incident: The restart loop"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Google SRE book: automation","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/automation-at-google/"},{"t":"Ansible documentation","u":"https://docs.ansible.com/"}],"text":"Runbooks are the natural first automation target because the steps are already known. The danger is acting on bad information, so safe automation is mostly about checks, limits and the ability to undo. What you will learn The ladder from manual steps to auto-remediation Preconditions and independent signals Dry runs, rate limits and circuit breakers Logging, rollback and review The ladder Documented: a clear runbook that people follow. Scripted: a tested script a person runs. Suggested: the system proposes the action and a person approves. Automatic: low-risk, well-understood actions run by themselves with limits. Only move up when the previous step has run reliably for a while. A guarded action import sys def restart_unhealthy ( service, state, dry_run= True ): if not state.metrics_available(): # do not act on missing data return \"skip: metrics unavailable\" unhealthy = state.unhealthy_instances(service) if len (unhealthy) > 0.2 * state.total(service): # too many: likely a wider problem return \"skip: more than 20% unhealthy, page a human\" if state.restarts_last_hour(service) >= 3 : # rate limit return \"skip: restart limit reached\" for i in unhealthy[: 1 ]: # one at a time print ( \"would restart\" if dry_run else \"restarting\" , i) if not dry_run: state.restart(i) return \"ok\" Guardrails Preconditions use several independent signals, never one. Blast radius limits: one instance at a time and a maximum per hour. Circuit breaker: stop automation after repeated failures. Dry run mode for testing, and audit log for every action. Rollback or a clear manual undo step. Review automated actions weekly and delete the ones nobody trusts. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Runbook automation and guardrails fits together Flow in order: 1, The ladder to A guarded action; 2, A guarded action to Guardrails. Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. The ladder Step What it does: Documented: a clear runbook that people follow. If it fails or is weak: Skipping dry runs. A guarded action Step What it does: . If it fails or is weak: No limit on how many things change at once. Guardrails Step What it does: Preconditions use several independent signals, never one. If it fails or is weak: Automation with no audit log. Incident: The restart loop Incident Auto-restart hit a healthy fleet when the metrics system failed. metrics: no data for 6 minutes automation: instances unhealthy: 100% restarted 48 of 48 instances The script read missing data as failure and restarted everything, causing the outage it was meant to prevent. The team added a data-available check, a 20 percent limit, a one-at-a-time rule and a circuit breaker that pages a human. Common mistakes Acting on a single signal No limit on how many things change at once Skipping dry runs Automation with no audit log"}});